@page "/ai-integration"

<PageTitle>AI on this site · Muhammad Kashif-Khan</PageTitle>

@* What's true now: the assistant (DeepSeekAgentService, AssistantContext) and the MCP server (McpService) *@
<article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pb-16 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-10 font-mono text-xs text-slate-400">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-cy">home</a>
        <span class="mx-1.5">/</span>
        <span class="text-slate-300">ai integration</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="mb-12">
        <p class="mono-label mb-3">// this website · AI</p>
        <h1 class="mb-5 max-w-4xl text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl">AI on this site</h1>
        <p class="max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
            There's an assistant you can ask about my work, and an MCP server so your own AI agent can do the same: search my projects, read my profile, or pass me a message.
        </p>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-8">
        <ProjectSection Title="The assistant" Icon="fas fa-robot">
            <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                <button type="button" onclick="mkChat.toggle()" class="text-cy hover:underline">Ask AI</button>, in the header, opens it. It runs on DeepSeek Flash and knows what this site knows:
                my experience, what I'm building and every project. It looks projects up through the same MCP tools listed below, and links to the pages it's talking about.
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-300">
                <li>• It can pass a message on to me: it asks for your name, email, a subject and the message, then sends it.</li>
                <li>• It only states what's in that data, and says so when it doesn't know. It can still get things wrong, so the project pages are the final word.</li>
            </ul>
        </ProjectSection>

        <ProjectSection Title="Connect your own agent" Subtitle="Streamable HTTP, no sign-in needed" Icon="fas fa-plug">
            <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">Point any MCP client at:</p>
            <pre class="mb-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-ink p-4 font-mono text-sm text-cy"><code>https://mkkai.dev/api/mcp</code></pre>
            <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                Clients only need that URL; each has its own way of adding it.
            </p>
            <div class="space-y-4">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="mono-label mb-2">// claude code</h3>
                    <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-ink p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-300"><code>claude mcp add --transport http mkkai https://mkkai.dev/api/mcp</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="mono-label mb-2">// cursor · ~/.cursor/mcp.json</h3>
                    <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-ink p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-300"><code>{ "mcpServers": { "mkkai": { "url": "https://mkkai.dev/api/mcp" } } }</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="mono-label mb-2">// vs code · .vscode/mcp.json</h3>
                    <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-ink p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-300"><code>{ "servers": { "mkkai": { "type": "http", "url": "https://mkkai.dev/api/mcp" } } }</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </ProjectSection>

        <ProjectSection Title="What it offers" Icon="fas fa-toolbox">
            <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="mono-label mb-3">// tools</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-300">
                        <li><code class="font-mono text-cy">search_projects</code>: find projects by <code class="font-mono">keyword</code>, <code class="font-mono">category</code> (Personal, Professional, Academic) or <code class="font-mono">technology</code>. Read-only, and returns structured results with each project's page.</li>
                        <li><code class="font-mono text-cy">submit_contact</code>: send me a message. Needs <code class="font-mono">name</code>, a valid <code class="font-mono">email</code>, <code class="font-mono">subject</code> and <code class="font-mono">message</code>; <code class="font-mono">company</code> and <code class="font-mono">role</code> are optional. A few messages an hour at most.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="mono-label mb-3">// resources</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 font-mono text-sm text-slate-300">
                        <li><span class="text-cy">portfolio://profile</span>: experience, what I'm building, stack</li>
                        <li><span class="text-cy">portfolio://about</span>: background and education</li>
                        <li><span class="text-cy">portfolio://contact</span>: how to reach me</li>
                        <li><span class="text-cy">portfolio://ai-integration</span>: this page, as data</li>
                        <li><span class="text-cy">portfolio://projects/all</span>: every project</li>
                        <li><span class="text-cy">portfolio://projects/{id}</span>: one project, named after its page, such as <span class="text-cy">hireforge</span></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </ProjectSection>

        <ProjectSection Title="Try it" Icon="fas fa-terminal">
            <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">List the tools, then search for Python projects:</p>
            <pre class="mb-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-ink p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-300"><code>curl -X POST https://mkkai.dev/api/mcp \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list"}'

curl -X POST https://mkkai.dev/api/mcp \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call",
       "params":{"name":"search_projects","arguments":{"technology":"Python"}}}'</code></pre>
            <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                Each reply comes back as a single event: an <code class="font-mono text-sm">event: message</code> line, then the JSON on a <code class="font-mono text-sm">data:</code> line.
            </p>
            <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                Or browse it with the MCP Inspector: run <code class="rounded bg-white/[.06] px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-cy">npx @@modelcontextprotocol/inspector</code> and connect to the URL above.
            </p>
        </ProjectSection>

        <ProjectSection Title="How it's built" Icon="fas fa-gears">
            <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                Both are part of this site's ASP.NET Core server. The MCP server is built on the official MCP C# SDK and follows the current spec (2026-07-28): stateless
                Streamable HTTP, with annotations that tell clients which tool only reads and which sends a message. The chat assistant runs the same tools in the same process,
                so it and an outside agent see exactly the same data, and the profile is built from the same content as the homepage, so the two can't disagree.
                There's more about the site itself on <a href="/projects/portfolio" class="text-cy hover:underline">its project page</a>.
            </p>
        </ProjectSection>
    </div>
</article>
